WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.769
I'm about 30km outside Reykjavík, in Iceland.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:08.350
And yes, earlier today, I was swimming in
power plant wastewater.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.040
Which is not something I'd usually recommend.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.430
If you were at a coal-fired power plant,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.780
well, even if you were just
swimming in the water

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.260
that had been boiled to spin the turbines
and then cooled back down again,

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.720
well, there are enough stories about
coal ash contamination,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.539
with arsenic and mercury and selenium,
that it would not be safe.

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:27.730
For gas and nuclear plants,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.240
as long as the water's
treated on the way out,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.500
you'd probably be fine? I think?

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.940
It's one of those things where I wouldn't
want to chance it.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.750
But that blue-white water is in the Blue Lagoon.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.280
Now, the management there are very careful
about how they phrase it.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.880
They prefer to talk about the
skin care and health benefits

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.890
that they claim come from
the silica in the water, but...

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.290
yeah, it's geothermal power plant wastewater.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:56.010
Okay, a quick diversion here: most cities
in the world have a cold water mains supply.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.399
There's a grid of cold water to all
the houses and buildings,

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:01.880
and it's heated on-demand when it's needed
in each building.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.910
But Iceland is geologically active:

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.090
in parts of the country, there are
high-pressure reservoirs of hot water

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.340
not too far underground,
where underground rivers meet hot rocks.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.929
In some places, those reservoirs break through
and reach the surface naturally.

00:01:13.929 --> 00:01:18.829
So near those places, it's economical to drill
a borehole down into the ground

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:23.229
and capture the superheated, high-pressure
steam and water that come back up.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:26.639
There are two things you can do with that
hot water and steam.

00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:27.909
It's not drinkable, first of all,

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:29.969
it's filled with silica and other bits of
dissolved rock,

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:32.689
but you can put it in a heat exchanger,

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:35.049
and use it to heat up fresh, clean cold water,

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:37.749
and then you can send the resulting
clean hot water

00:01:37.749 --> 00:01:39.679
down a pipeline to paying customers.

00:01:39.679 --> 00:01:41.590
And this is one of those pipelines!

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.999
There is hot water speeding
through this to Reykjavík.

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:47.409
It doesn't actually feel warm,
if it did the pipe wouldn't be doing its job,

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:49.929
because the water only loses a couple of degrees
over dozens of kilometres,

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:52.289
but yes, hot water speeding through that.

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:55.929
Icelandic cities have separate
hot water and cold water grids.

00:01:55.929 --> 00:01:58.460
If your home is connected to that grid you
don't need a water heater.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.090
So, okay, that's hot water.
If you want electricity instead,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.799
you can spin a turbine using the
high-pressure steam that's coming up,

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:07.810
and generate clean power that way.
Technically, you can do both,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.170
but pipelines are expensive and there's a
lot of hot water around here,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.840
so often it's just electricity or hot water.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.970
Even a massive, industrial-size geothermal
borehole will last a few decades

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.379
before it starts to run out of heat,
and when that happens,

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:22.760
you can just open another one
a few kilometres away.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.760
The old one will get back up to temperature
sooner or later,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.610
the Earth is not going to run out of heat.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:32.019
Which means Iceland has a
massive supply of hot water.

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:37.510
High up on a hill above Reykjavík, there's
this beautiful building called Perlan.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.159
It's got exhibitions about the
natural wonders of Iceland

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:42.209
and a revolving restaurant
and an observation deck.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:46.180
But the big round things on the outside aren't
part of the exhibitions:

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.940
they're the enormous hot water storage tanks
that the building was built around.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:53.469
Perlan is a water tower, just like any other
you'd find around the world,

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:55.519
except it stores hot water.

00:02:55.519 --> 00:02:59.999
There's so much of it that,
while it's not free, it's very very cheap.

00:02:59.999 --> 00:03:03.170
If your house is a bit too warm in winter
in Iceland, you don't turn the heating down:

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.010
you just open a window and
let some fresh air in.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.489
Iceland also has quite a lot of fresh air.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:13.069
When it's snowing, the pavements are heated
up by hot water pipes underneath.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:14.069
Last time I was here was in winter,

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.160
the lake behind City Hall was frozen over,
the ice was strong enough to safely walk on.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.290
In one corner, there's a pipe constantly pouring
a bit hot water into that lake,

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.530
just so the ducks have somewhere to swim.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.799
And yes, there are natural hot springs
elsewhere in Iceland,

00:03:27.799 --> 00:03:30.150
there are natural hot spring beaches
elsewhere in the world,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.579
but Reykjavík has an
artificial hot water beach:

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:36.739
they just pump hot water into that lagoon
to heat it up a bit.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:39.140
There's a hot tub down there!

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.480
But this isn't power plant wastewater.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.269
It's regular water, like you'd get out of
the cold tap in your home,

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:49.370
just heated up in a heat exchanger by the
high-pressure undrinkable water and steam

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.629
that comes up from the boreholes
at the geothermal power plants.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:53.040
The two never touch.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.420
So there's one loose end here:

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.719
after that high-pressure steam comes up to
the surface and gets used…

00:03:57.719 --> 00:03:59.200
where does it go?

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.580
Well, once it's cooled down a bit,
the geothermal plants

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.970
just dump the water out onto nearby rocks.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.269
Those rocks are permeable,
the water slowly sinks through them,

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:10.380
and over decades it will make its way back
to where it came from.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.590
Mostly.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.830
When you're dumping that much water out, though,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.160
it will tend to form pools and… lagoons.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.669
Particularly when it's got
a lot of silica in it.

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:21.190
And the story from there is simple:

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.319
someone went bathing in one of those pools,
thought it helped their skin condition,

00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:26.910
and a few decades of construction
and marketing later

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.330
the Blue Lagoon is one of the
"wonders of the world"

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.771
and they charge more than
€30 for a basic ticket.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.110
And I don't want to knock that too much!

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.169
Because yes, there is some research that says
bathing in geothermal water like that

00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:41.300
helps some skin conditions,
and there is a big difference

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.660
between wild swimming in a
mysterious hot-water pool

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.180
and bathing in a well-regulated
geothermal spa

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.139
that gives you a towel and
offers in-water massages.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:53.710
But if you feel like being cynical, then yes,

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:59.680
that is power plant wastewater.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.248
Whew.

00:05:04.142 --> 00:05:06.828
Iceland is beautiful.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.340
Bit wet. But beautiful.

